    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="definition">The definition</h2>
      <p>
        Artometrics is a practice at the intersection of art and science. It asks a simple question
        with a hard method: if you looked at a painting, a pop catalogue, a franchise, or a style
        the way a scientist looks at a dataset — and kept the artist's eye while you did it — what
        would you actually learn?
      </p>
      <p>
        The name is literal. <em>Art</em> is the subject and the sensibility. <em>Metrics</em> are
        the instruments: counts, timelines, sources, comparisons, and the honesty to say when the
        record is thin. The point is not to replace taste with a number. The point is to make taste
        accountable to evidence, and to make evidence visible enough that someone else can follow it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="not">What it is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not a ranking engine, and it is not a claim that beauty can be solved. A list of
        "top" works without a method is just a mood. A model that cannot explain its own cuts is
        just a vibe with extra steps. Artometrics starts from the opposite habit: show the data,
        show the sources, and show the limits of both.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also not a requirement that you become a specialist in every field you touch.
        Most people cannot spend a life on art history and another on statistics. This Academy
        exists because of that constraint. We collect the shortest useful path through the
        intersection — the ideas that actually changed how we see the work.
      </p>

      <h2 id="practice">How the practice looks</h2>
      <p>
        In practice it looks like a magazine article with a lab notebook behind it. You might
        count how a style spreads, reconstruct a career from the few reliable sources, or put a
        canonical biography next to the event that actually formed the work. The Glossary next
        door is the encyclopedia version of that habit: not a copy of Wikipedia's opening
        paragraph, but an expert, modern-context reading — who the person was, how they lived,
        and why the work looks the way it does.
      </p>
      <p>
        Leonardo is a useful example of the difference. The usual entry will tell you he was a
        Renaissance polymath. An Artometrics entry starts from the science inside the art: the
        anatomy, the study of water, the smoky paint that caught light like a photograph before
        photography existed — and a sources note about how little written record we actually have.
      </p>

      <h2 id="start">Where to start</h2>
      <p>
        Start here, then branch. Read one guide for the method, one Glossary entry for a person,
        and one style board for a visual language you can reuse. When the full library is filled
        in, the promise is the same: here are the things we've found, so you can get farther than
        we did.
      </p>
